WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.892
Nobody trusted the numbers.

2
00:00:02.737 --> 00:00:06.761
70+ dashboards, 3-5 leadership escalations a month,

3
00:00:06.822 --> 00:00:09.273
and stakeholders double-checking every figure.

4
00:00:09.701 --> 00:00:12.039
Patching dashboards wasn't going to fix that.

5
00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:14.720
So I split the team's effort in two:

6
00:00:14.921 --> 00:00:16.734
long-term strategic reporting,

7
00:00:16.819 --> 00:00:18.922
and self-service for ad-hoc requests.

8
00:00:19.444 --> 00:00:21.654
Then we backed it with single-source-of-truth views,

9
00:00:21.761 --> 00:00:23.934
refresh schedules aligned to the warehouse,

10
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.191
and tripwires to catch drift early.

11
00:00:26.647 --> 00:00:28.972
Closing the trust gap took just as much work:

12
00:00:29.022 --> 00:00:30.697
plain-English data catalogues,

13
00:00:30.781 --> 00:00:32.618
dashboards with a clear narrative,

14
00:00:32.749 --> 00:00:34.204
agreed measure definitions,

15
00:00:34.277 --> 00:00:35.975
and data literacy sessions,

16
00:00:36.071 --> 00:00:38.012
so people could challenge the numbers.

17
00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:43.692
The result? KPI consistency went from 62% to 100%,

18
00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:47.813
duplicate and end-of-life dashboards fell from 70+ to 46,

19
00:00:48.025 --> 00:00:49.653
escalations dropped to zero,

20
00:00:49.738 --> 00:00:52.928
and workspace health climbed from 60% to 93%.

21
00:00:53.359 --> 00:00:54.523
It's held ever since,

22
00:00:54.619 --> 00:00:58.377
with tripwire alerts still watching for variance above 2%.

23
00:00:58.834 --> 00:01:01.282
That's trusted executive KPI metrics.
